# HomerView
#
# Logs are written on every run and say nothing a reader of the repository
# needs. They are also the one file most likely to carry a local path.
*.log

# Generated at run time, all of them rewritten on the next run.
Axe.json
Ace.json
Report.html
Report.txt
Start.html
MainContent.htm
HomerView.db
HomerView.jsonl

# Private correspondence. Drafts live outside the repository.
Letter_to_*.md
Transition_*.md
Edge_Hotkeys.md
Reply_to_*.md
Announcing_*.md
Announcing_*.htm
Letter_to_*.htm

# Scripts that belong to this machine rather than to the project. The
# repository script exists to create the repository, so a clone has no use for
# it; the clean script is a local convenience. Both stay here and are not
# pushed. The two Git configuration files above are the exception: they
# describe the repository and belong in it.
clean.cmd
createHomerViewRepo.cmd
createHomerViewRepo.ps1
createHomerViewRepo.log
cleanDir.log

# Python and build output.
__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.pyo
dist/

# Executables are tracked deliberately, so someone who clones this can build
# the installer without hunting for pieces. There is nothing to write here for
# that: they are tracked because nothing excludes them. The two lines that used
# to sit here re-including them did nothing at all, because an exclamation mark
# only re-includes what an earlier pattern excluded.
#
# Two exceptions.
#
# pandoc.exe is about 220 megabytes and GitHub refuses any single file over a
# hundred. It is no longer packaged at all: the installer offers to fetch it
# on the last page, the way HomerScribe fetches Ollama. A local copy here is
# ignored rather than committed.
pandoc.exe
installPandoc.log

# The built installer, which is a release asset rather than source. It is
# rebuilt from the setup script by anyone who needs it, and committing a
# hundred megabyte binary on every release would make the repository
# unpleasant to clone.
HomerView_setup.exe
